<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 20th century, after the Iranian Constitutional Revolution; first sung in public <a href="/years/1924">1924</a>; first recorded <a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as مرغ سحر, Morgh-e Sahar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Morq-e Sahar&quot; is a tasnif, a Persian art song, with words by the poet Mohammad-Taqi Bahar and music by Morteza Neidavoud. It was made in the early 20th century in the wake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a>, and it has often been sung in protest against injustice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and tyranny in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; it is often called the &quot;anthem&quot; of Iran's struggles for freedom.</p></div>
